  <h1 id="top">Meat for Beef Jerky in a Dehydrator: Which Cut to Use (Ranked)</h1>

  <div class="answer-box">
    <p>The best <strong>meat for beef jerky in a dehydrator</strong> is <strong>eye of round</strong> — it's lean (about 4% fat), uniform in shape for easy slicing, and priced well. Top round, bottom round, and flank steak are strong runners-up. Trim all visible fat, slice ⅛-inch thick against the grain, and dehydrate at 160°F for 4–6 hours.</p>
  </div>

  <ContentImage src="/images/meat-for-beef-jerky-in-dehydrator-hero.webp" alt="A lean eye of round roast next to freshly sliced beef strips ready to become dehydrator jerky on a wooden kitchen counter" eager={true} />

  <p>I have made jerky in dehydrators for fifteen years, and the question I get asked more than any other is which cut of meat to use. The internet has plenty of "best meat for beef jerky" articles, but almost none of them ask the more specific question: <strong>which cut is best for the dehydrator method specifically</strong>? <em>[Switches to serious face]</em> The dehydrator rewards certain qualities — leanness, uniform thickness, low marbling, cuts that hold up to 6 hours of low airflow — that a smoker or oven doesn't care about as much. This is the honest ranking, the time each cut takes, and a foolproof recipe built around the winner.</p>

