      <tr><td><strong>Ribeye / chuck</strong></td><td>~20%+</td><td>Poor for jerky</td><td>☆☆☆☆☆</td><td>Do not. Save for steak.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <ContentImage src="/images/meat-for-beef-jerky-in-dehydrator-cuts-comparison.webp" alt="Four labeled beef cuts side by side on a wooden cutting board — eye of round, top round, bottom round, and flank steak — showing the visual differences between the best meat choices for jerky in a dehydrator" />

  <p>For deeper coverage of cuts in general (across all jerky methods, not just dehydrator), see our <a href="/best-meat-for-beef-jerky/">best meat for beef jerky</a> guide — this pillar is the dehydrator-specific narrower cut.</p>

  <h2 id="what-meat-to-use-for-beef-jerky-in-a-dehydrator">What Meat to Use for Beef Jerky in a Dehydrator</h2>
  <p>If you are standing at the grocery store trying to decide <strong>what meat to use for beef jerky in a dehydrator</strong> right now, here is the decision tree:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>On a budget?</strong> Eye of round. Every time.</li>
    <li><strong>Want more flavor and don't mind spending a little more?</strong> Top round.</li>
    <li><strong>Making it for a party where texture matters?</strong> Bottom round. Its slightly tougher grain gives that satisfying chew people expect.</li>
    <li><strong>Flank steak is on sale?</strong> Grab it. Great flavor, dries beautifully.</li>
    <li><strong>All you can find is 80/20 ground beef?</strong> You need a jerky gun and Prague Powder cure. Different path — see the <a href="/jerky-gun/">jerky gun guide</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>All you can find is brisket, chuck, or ribeye?</strong> Walk out and go to a different store. These do not work in a dehydrator.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Buy 2 to 2.5 pounds of raw beef per pound of finished jerky you want. The dehydration process removes 50 to 60 percent of the raw weight as water.</p>

  <h2 id="how-lean-should-the-meat-be-for-dehydrator-jerky">How Lean Should the Meat Be for Dehydrator Jerky</h2>
  <p>Under 10% fat is the practical maximum. Under 5% is ideal. This rule is universal across every credible jerky guide including the <a href="https://ohioline.osu.edu/factsheet/hyg-5362" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Ohio State University Extension jerky fact sheet</a>. The reason: <strong>fat does not evaporate in a dehydrator</strong>. It melts, pools on the surface, and eventually goes rancid — dramatically shortening shelf life and making the finished jerky greasy.</p>
  <p>Trim every visible bit of fat from the outside of the roast BEFORE slicing. Fat trapped inside a strip cannot be removed after; fat on the outside can. This one step probably matters more than which specific cut you pick.</p>

  <h2 id="how-to-slice-meat-for-dehydrator-jerky">How to Slice Meat for Dehydrator Jerky</h2>
  <p>The slicing step is where amateur jerky becomes professional-looking jerky. Two rules:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Partial-freeze the roast.</strong> 45 to 60 minutes in the freezer until firm to the touch but not solid. Room-temperature meat tears when you try to slice it thin; frozen meat crushes under the knife. Partial-frozen meat cuts cleanly.</li>
    <li><strong>Slice against the grain for tender jerky, with the grain for chewy.</strong> Against the grain (perpendicular to the muscle fibers) gives a jerky that breaks apart cleanly when you bite it — my preference. With the grain gives that pull-and-chew traditional-cowboy texture some people love. Neither is wrong.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Target 1/8 inch thick for standard dehydrator jerky. Any thinner and it turns into leather; any thicker and it does not dry evenly by hour 6.</p>

  <ContentImage src="/images/meat-for-beef-jerky-in-dehydrator-slicing.webp" alt="Sharp chef knife slicing a partial-frozen eye of round roast against the grain into 1/8 inch strips on a wooden cutting board" />

