  <p>For the deeper how-to on running a dehydrator (temperature settings by brand, tray rotation, doneness tests), our <a href="/dehydrator-beef-jerky/">dehydrator beef jerky</a> pillar is the reference. This page focuses on the meat side of the equation.</p>

  <h2 id="what-temperature-to-dehydrate-beef-jerky">What Temperature to Dehydrate Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p><strong>Dehydrate beef jerky at 160°F</strong>. This is the USDA-recommended temperature for whole-muscle jerky to reach the pathogen kill step for E. coli and Salmonella. The <a href="https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/meat/jerky-and-food-safety" rel="noopener" target="_blank">USDA FSIS Jerky and Food Safety guide</a> is the definitive reference; anything below 145°F is unsafe. If your dehydrator only reaches 145°F, finish the strips in a 275°F oven for 10 minutes to hit the safety target after the dehydrator does the drying work.</p>
  <p>Cheaper dehydrators sometimes lack adjustable temperature; if yours doesn't, verify with a bi-metal thermometer inside the unit at the highest setting. If the unit does not sustain 160°F, either add the oven finish step every batch or upgrade — see our <a href="/best-dehydrator-for-jerky/">best dehydrator for jerky</a> guide for models that reliably hit the safety target.</p>

  <h2 id="how-long-each-cut-takes-in-a-dehydrator">How Long Each Cut Takes in a Dehydrator</h2>
  <p>This is the table nobody publishes but should. Different cuts take meaningfully different drying times at 160°F, even at the same 1/8-inch slice thickness. My observed times running the same dehydrator (Excalibur 3926TB) across dozens of batches:</p>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Cut (1/8" slices)</th><th>Approx. dry time at 160°F</th><th>Why</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Eye of round</strong></td><td>4–5 hours</td><td>Very lean; dries fastest.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Top round</strong></td><td>4.5–5.5 hours</td><td>Slightly more marbling holds moisture a touch longer.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Bottom round</strong></td><td>5–6 hours</td><td>Denser muscle grain.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Flank steak</strong></td><td>5–6 hours</td><td>Grain structure retains moisture.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Sirloin tip</strong></td><td>5.5–6.5 hours</td><td>Marginally fattier; slower dry.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Ground beef (90/10) through jerky gun</strong></td><td>5–7 hours</td><td>Denser packed strips; longer dry.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>These are guides, not laws. Your specific dehydrator's airflow pattern, ambient humidity, and how tightly you loaded the trays will shift these by 30 to 60 minutes either way. <strong>Always check by feel starting at hour 4</strong>: pull one strip out, let it cool for two minutes, and bend it. If it cracks along the fibers but does not snap, it is done.</p>

  <h2 id="ground-beef-vs-whole-muscle-in-a-dehydrator">Ground Beef vs Whole Muscle in a Dehydrator</h2>
  <p>Whole-muscle jerky (sliced eye of round, top round, etc.) is what most people picture, but ground beef jerky is a genuinely legitimate parallel path. Here is the honest comparison for the dehydrator method specifically:</p>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Whole muscle (sliced)</th><th>Ground beef (jerky gun)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Cost per finished ounce</td><td>Higher — cuts of round cost more per lb</td><td>Lower — 90/10 ground beef is cheaper</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Texture</td><td>Traditional chew, muscle-fiber grain</td><td>Tender, uniform, easier for kids/older folks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Prep effort</td><td>Slice + marinate</td><td>Mix + load gun + extrude</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Shelf life (cured)</td><td>Longer — less surface area exposed to bacteria</td><td>Shorter — grinding spreads surface bacteria through batch</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cure requirement</td><td>Recommended, not strictly required</td><td>Strongly required (Prague Powder #1)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Uniformity</td><td>Depends on slicing skill</td><td>Excellent — jerky gun makes identical strips</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Dehydrator time at 160°F</td><td>4–6 hours</td><td>5–7 hours</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>My take: whole muscle first, ground beef second. Ground beef becomes the right choice when you cannot get lean cuts, when you want to make snack sticks, or when you are batching for a group and want uniform pieces. See our <a href="/jerky-gun/">jerky gun guide</a> for the ground-beef technique end-to-end. Either path uses the same dehydrator at the same 160°F.</p>

